DESCRIPTION: Overgrown - Modified Lecture Particle system (Wakefield,
https://www.shadertoy.com/view/7ff3RX) and Ising cellular automata model
(Wakefield, https://www.shadertoy.com/view/33yyzc) aim to show an ability how
two systems can modify each other with the states creating generative textures
based on their internal states and randomness. The particle system follows the
probability map of the Ising model morphed by the perlin noise (Gustavson,
https://gist.github.com/patriciogonzalezvivo/670c22f3966e662d2f83). The automata
based on the timing records random values of the wandering particles saved in
the texture and use it to create a new generated by the particles probability
field. This cycle of generative texture loops over.

It's definitely interesting to see the particle, perlin noise and automata
interactions. Each phase the particles wander around the static probability
field and then are being sucked by the perlin noise field to once again get on
the probability field tracks. Then the random overgrowth happens and the
particles form a new probability field by randomly wandering around. When I was
doing this work it was resembling a blood vessels in the leaf (new way to create
a heart tissue)(National Geographic, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4KS6NyFo3Y) 
The growth is similar to some of the plant growth timelapses that I was watching 
before the project (timelapselop, https://www.youtube.com/shorts/6rr6xd9-Ad4). 
The system doesn't create a long term behaviour but rather generative texture 
within the 32 seconds phase. Despite that the effect is quite different after the initial phase.

